From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 17:16:52 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] SecureElement: start the process when an application binds
 the service

SecureElement is the OMAPI service for an embedded secure element or a
UICC reader. The application is persistent, so the system starts its
process at every boot and the application starts the service from onCreate;
the process then holds about 5.7 MiB at idle whether or not an SEService
client ever asks for it. A device without a reader has no use for that: its
clients find no reader, but they still have to connect.

SEService looks for the system's secure_element service first and, when
the service is missing, binds the application's SecureElementService
intent. Remove android:persistent, so nothing starts the process at boot
and the first such bind does: the application's onCreate then starts and
registers the service exactly as before, and the process is one that the
system may reclaim under memory pressure. Nothing else about the
application changes.
---
 AndroidManifest.xml | 1 -
 1 file changed, 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/AndroidManifest.xml b/AndroidManifest.xml
index 9e3a75bb8b90610cb94392f82eece45045106c10..d2034975b60f8059f7cc057d6ac26673dfee42f0 100755
--- a/AndroidManifest.xml
+++ b/AndroidManifest.xml
@@ -7,7 +7,6 @@
     <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES"/>
     <application android:name=".SEApplication"
          android:label="SecureElementApplication"
-         android:persistent="true"
          android:directBootAware="true"
          android:defaultToDeviceProtectedStorage="true"
          android:memtagMode="async">
